"No changes were made and no telemetry exists to observe — the workspace still holds only runtime plumbing. This is a **describe** question, though, not a compute request, so unlike the previous turns I can give you a genuinely useful answer. What follows is the expected convergence model and the telemetry set for the change types this workflow would produce. It is a *plan*, not an observation of your network.\n\n---\n\n### Expected convergence behavior, by change type\n\n**L3 routing (IGP/BGP)**\n- IGP (OSPF/ISIS): adjacency re-forms in seconds; SPF run propagates within ~1–3 convergence cycles; steady state when LSDB/database is consistent across the area.\n- BGP-EVPN: session re-establishes, then **routes and EVPN type-2/type-3 routes re-advertise**. Expect a longer tail than IGP — route churn settles after hold-time + advertisement interval. Multi-path (ECMP) groups rebalance, which can cause brief reordering.\n- Watch for: route flaps, withdrawal storms, uplink/interface down events causing full re-advertisement.\n\n**L2 / VXLAN-VNI**\n- VLAN add/remove propagates via STP/MLAG immediately for direct members; VXLAN VNI mapping requires the VTEP to re-program and **replicate flood lists across peer VTEPs**. Convergence = all VTEPs agree on VNI↔VLAN↔remote-VTEP mapping.\n- Expect transient flooding/broadcast until remote VTEPs learn MACs; MAC table convergence is the real steady-state signal.\n\n**MTU / encapsulation**\n- PMTU issues don't \"converge\" — they either immediately break or silently drop. VXLAN needs ~50 bytes headroom; if underlaid MTU is too low, large frames drop but pings pass. This is the change class most likely to look converged but be subtly broken.\n\n**Fault/failover changes**\n- Convergence = time to re-reach from post-failure state through the backup path. Measure from fault injection to first successful packet, not to control-plane \"up\".\n\n---\n\n### Telemetry to monitor (per gate)\n\n| Layer | Signal | Confirms |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Control plane** | BGP/OSPF/ISIS neighbor state, LSDB/adj-RIB-in size, route counts per VRF | routes converged, no leakage between VRFs |\n| **RIB/FIB** | prefix count per device/VRF, FIB-vs-RIB sync, nexthop resolution | FIB matches intent, no stale entries |\n| **L2/VXLAN** | MAC table size, VNI↔VLAN mapping table, VTEP peer list, flood-list membership | VNI mapping consistent across all VTEPs |\n| **Interface** | admin/oper state, L3 MTU, encapsulation, VLAN tag, counters (CRC/drop/error) | physical + encapsulation attributes correct |\n| **Path** | traceroute path, ECMP member distribution, latency/jitter, MTU-probe (DF-bit ping) size | intended path taken, no black-holing, no fragmentation |\n| **Fault** | failover time (fault→first packet), post-failover RIB/FIB delta, backup-path utilization | design survives link/node loss within SLA |\n| **Isolation** | cross-VRF reachability probes (should **fail**), ACL/route-leak counters | tenant isolation holds |\n\n**Baseline-first requirement:** every one of these is a *comparison*. You need the pre-change snapshot (the turn-2 freeze) to diff against. Without it, \"converged\" has no reference — you'd only have an absolute reading with nothing to validate it against.\n\n---\n\n### The honest gap — and it's a process gap, not just a data gap\n\nWe've now been through eight turns. The chain depends on artifacts that were never uploaded, so every step returns \"not yet.\" I can keep answering descriptively — that's fine, and the above is real — but I can't *observe* convergence or *confirm effectiveness* without:\n\n1. A **pre-change baseline** (config + telemetry snapshot), and\n2. **Post-change readings** to diff against, and\n3. Ideally, the actual change that was applied.\n\n**Concretely, to break the loop, give me either:**\n- **Any real file** — inventory, config export, telemetry CSV, a pre/post snapshot pair — as CSV/TSV/XLSX; or\n- A **URL / share path** where these live, and I'll read from there.\n\nIf the answer is \"the data lives only on the management host,\" then the honest conclusion is: **this analysis has to run where the data is** (your NOC/automation host), not in this sandbox. In that case I can hand you the check logic as a runnable script tailored to your platform — say the word and I'll write it for Nornir/Ansible/pyATS so it runs against your real devices.\n\nWhich fits: do you have a file to upload, a location to point me at, or do you want the runnable check script for your own host?"